1: FluentValidation is validation library for .NET that uses a fluent interface
2: and lambda expressions for building strongly-typed validation rules.
3: 
4: ### Supporting the project
5: 
6: If you use FluentValidation in a commercial project,
7: please sponsor the project financially.
8: FluentValidation is developed and supported by [@JeremySkinner](https://github.com/JeremySkinner)
9: for free in his spare time and financial sponsorship helps keep the project going.
10: You can sponsor the project via either [GitHub sponsors](https://github.com/sponsors/JeremySkinner) or [OpenCollective](https://opencollective.com/FluentValidation).
11: 
12: ### Example
13: 
14: With FluentValidation, you can define a class that inherits from `AbstractValidator`
15: which contains the rules for a particular class. The example below shows how you could define rules
16: for a `Customer` class, and then how to execute the validator.
17: 
18: ```c#
19: using FluentValidation;
20: 
21: public class CustomerValidator: AbstractValidator<Customer> {
22:   public CustomerValidator() {
23:     RuleFor(x => x.Surname).NotEmpty();
24:     RuleFor(x => x.Forename).NotEmpty().WithMessage("Please specify a first name");
25:     RuleFor(x => x.Discount).NotEqual(0).When(x => x.HasDiscount);
26:     RuleFor(x => x.Address).Length(20, 250);
27:     RuleFor(x => x.Postcode).Must(BeAValidPostcode).WithMessage("Please specify a valid postcode");
28:   }
29: 
30:   private bool BeAValidPostcode(string postcode) {
31:     // custom postcode validating logic goes here
32:   }
33: }
34: 
35: var customer = new Customer();
36: var validator = new CustomerValidator();
37: 
38: // Execute the validator.
39: ValidationResult results = validator.Validate(customer);
40: 
41: // Inspect any validation failures.
42: bool success = results.IsValid;
43: List<ValidationFailure> failures = results.Errors;
44: ```
45: 
46: ### Full Documentation
47: 
48: Full documentation can be found at
49: [https://docs.fluentvalidation.net](https://docs.fluentvalidation.net)
50: 
